reviewer.reply:
{
    "verdict": "reject",
    "blockers": [
        {
            "claim": "C ABI integrity violation: ecuio_init and ecuio_shutdown are declared in the public C API but are now no-op functions that do nothing, breaking the contract that callers expect these functions to have observable effect. The documentation says 'init always returns ECUIO_OK' but doesn't explain what the functions actually do or guarantee.",
            "file": "core/include/ecuio/core.h",
            "quote": "ecuio_status_t ecuio_init(void);\n void ecuio_shutdown(void);"
        },
        {
            "claim": "Thread safety violation: The global atomic g_initialized was removed from core.cpp, but the comment claims 'core holds no global state that needs them'. This is incorrect if there are any global resources that need initialization. The removal of g_initialized without ensuring all global state is properly handled is dangerous.",
            "file": "core/src/core.cpp",
            "quote": "ecuio_status_t ecuio_init(void)\n {\n     return ECUIO_OK;\n }"
        },
        {
            "claim": "Error propagation broken: The QML bridge previously used m_initialized to track whether ecuio_init succeeded. Now that ecuio_init is a no-op that always returns ECUIO_OK, the bridge has no way to know if initialization actually succeeded. The (void)ecuio_init() call discards the return value, eliminating error detection.",
            "file": "app/qttunebridge.cpp",
            "quote": "(void)ecuio_init();   // optional and always ECUIO_OK (core.h), paired below"
        }
    ],
    "questions": [
        "What global state, if any, does the core module actually have that requires initialization? The removal of g_initialized suggests none, but the original code had it for a reason.",
        "Are there any callers outside this repository that depend on ecuio_init having side effects (like initializing global resources, setting up logging, etc.)?",
        "What happens if ecuio_init is never called and a session is created? Does the profile registry or discovery code handle this case correctly?",
        "Is there any race condition in the profile registry initialization? The comment mentions 'C++11 magic statics' but I need to verify this is sufficient."
    ],
    "nits": [
        "The comment in core.h says 'init always returns ECUIO_OK' but should also document that the function does nothing (no side effects).",
        "The comment in core.cpp could be clearer about why ecuio_init and ecuio_shutdown are now no-ops.",
        "Consider adding a deprecation notice or documentation explaining the lifecycle change for existing callers."
    ]
}
